A||||8||BBBAD GT||Anybody have DSL?||||1||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||10-01-2000||11:00 PM||||GTE is running a special until 10/31, $40/mo with free modem and no setup charges with a one year contract.  I'm getting fed up with my dial up (even tho I just woke up to the 56k world just 4 months ago).  Anyway, its the bottom 700k download, 150k upload speed for the $40/mo, but I think I can live with that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Robert||10-02-2000||12:34 AM||||I have DSL at my shop, and it is faster than the cable modem I have at home.  The only caution is, DSL’s speed is based on how far from the controller doohickey box you are.  The further away from the box you are, the slower the speed. On the up side, the DSL line can be used as your phone line and modem at the same time.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||10-02-2000||02:29 AM||||A co worker has DSL, he is a hardcore PC gamer/downloader/programmer.  He is going to switch over to cacblemodem.  Although it is faster, he says it is not as stable as the cablemodem service in my area, and in general he is of the impression that since they capped his transfer rates the DSL sucks @ss compared to the local cablemodem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-02-2000||09:12 PM||||They can cap cable modem rates. They have in some areas. Check over the contract really carefull before doing that one.<P>Also know that cable modem effectivly puts you on a LAN. Unless carefully configured everyone in your area can see your machine and any shared resources. You are also open to hacking by local users.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||10-02-2000||10:20 PM||||Cable modem rates are almost always capped as well; AT&T/RoadRunner/MediaOne caps at 1.5Mbit incoming & 300Kbit outbound for example.  <P>DSL is getting better but because it isn't considered a business-class service you need to be prepared for up to a 1-3 day turnaround for problem resolution; keep a regular modem and a NetZero account in case you need dialup backup.  A lot of that is because of 3 things: 1) there's often more than 1 party involved in providing service (and when something breaks there's fingerpointing), 2) vendors are still working out kinks in their products, and 3) general shortage of clueful and qualified network engineers.  Cable modems aren't as bad because the technology is a bit more mature. <P>One other important thing to check in the contract is the policy on running servers on your machine; some DSL providers will void your contract if you run a mail or web server for example, even if they're clearly not being used for commercial purposes. <P>As far as security vulnerabilities, there isn't much difference between DSL and cable modem.  Maintaining proper security is ultimately your responsibility.<P>Lastly....if the best you can get is ISDL I feel for you.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||whamel||10-03-2000||08:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Cable modem rates are almost always capped as well; AT&T/RoadRunner/MediaOne caps at 1.5Mbit incoming & 300Kbit outbound for example.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine's capped a LOT less than that!  I haven't speed tested it, but I average speeds that are comparable with ISDN most of the time.  That's all Roadrunner says they will promise in my area.<BR>On the other hand - I have been waiting 3 months for my DSL to be installed at my other house.  Still no luck, still no word on when.<BR>Wade<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||10-03-2000||03:43 PM||||i love the offices DSL <A HREF="http://www.dslreports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dslreports.com</A>  says it's 68 times faster than 56k... but is suck to go home and get on that LAME 56k... ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||10-03-2000||11:47 PM||||I wish my cable had a cap like that! Mine is capped at about 25k outbound. It's still good enough for my Hotline server though!<P>And now they are trying this deal where you buy the $400 modem and them knock your rates down to $30 per month! But then who are you gonna call when the modem dies as mine did a few weeks ago? I'll stick with my $45 per month and their modem.||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||10-04-2000||11:31 AM||||That cap is what MediaOne was selling ever since they introduced the service.  My acutal measured maximums are about 1.73Mbit inbound and 320Kbit outbound. I don't run a commercial web server (its against the TOS anyways) so 30 kbytes/second is fine for what I use it for. I rent the modem too, they knock off $10/month if you buy one which is about a 2 year payback, to me not worth it. The model of cable modem I've got acutally supports about 8Mbit/second service, AT&T (they just bought RoadRunner/M1) throttles it back.  <P><BR>||||reg||1
